<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Batumi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Batum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Batumi is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and the capital of the Autonomous Republic of Adjara. Ottoman from the 16th century until 1878, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, it became an oil-export terminal at the end of the railway and pipeline from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>. Contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Georgia in 1918–1920, it passed to Soviet Georgia under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> in 1921.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batumi.jpg" alt="Batumi" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Commander, U.S. Naval Forces Europe-Africa/U.S. 6th Fleet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USS_Oak_Hill,_26th_MEU_Marines_Visit_Batumi,_Georgia_(40817303032).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
